const FIELD_NOTES=[{"id":"2026-05-20-hopkins-rose-marine-mammal-cart","date":"May 20, 2026 (Bay Watch May 14–20)","title":"Marine Mammal Cart Narrations • Hopkins’ Rose Nudibranch • Sea Slug Search","summary":["Marine Mammal Cart training and six prop narration guides with expandable visitor questions.","Species Highlight: Hopkins’ rose nudibranch (Ceratodoris rosacea), seven illustrated images.","Sea Slug Search 2026; Bay Watch May 14–20, 2026."],"observations":[{"title":"Marine Mammal Cart — shift operations and training questions","text":"Marine Mammal Cart training materials and the Naturalist Library marine mammal pages are recommended for guides. Guests may touch the models, but should not handle or lift them. Mentioning model weights is optional. If the cart must close mid-shift, the location for keys was not yet confirmed; specialists will be consulted. A request to make the black puck more visible will be forwarded. If a puck does not activate a video, move it slightly until the animal profile border turns yellow; if it still fails, contact a specialist when available and continue engaging guests with the remaining materials. Holley and Christy can assist with training questions. The 2024 advanced marine mammal class and Steve Webster’s gray whale skeleton story were also recommended."},{"title":"Marine Mammal Cart — prop-by-prop narration guide","text":"Select a prop to open its suggested visitor questions and interpretive talking points. The accompanying handout also provides Spanish wording. The guide below paraphrases the source; videos on the physical cart are not embedded.","props":[{"name":"Blue whale ear bone","intro":"Blue whales hear calls across immense distances.","questions":[{"q":"Why do male blue whales sing?","answer":"Male blue whales sing to find females over vast distances. Their low-frequency calls can travel up to 1,000 miles. Underwater noise from shipping can interfere with communication."},{"q":"Why do blue whales visit Monterey Bay?","answer":"They migrate thousands of miles from warm breeding waters to Monterey Bay to feed on dense krill swarms. An individual may eat thousands of pounds of krill daily."}],"spanish":"El hueso del oído de la ballena azul. Las ballenas azules pueden oír los llamados de otras a mil millas de distancia."},{"name":"Dolphin jaw","intro":"Dolphins receive sound vibrations through their lower jaws.","questions":[{"q":"How do dolphins find food in dark waters?","answer":"Dolphins emit high-pitched echolocation clicks; returning echoes travel through the lower jaw to the auditory system, allowing them to locate prey in poor visibility."},{"q":"How does teamwork help dolphins catch prey?","answer":"Pacific white-sided dolphins cooperate to herd schools of fish into dense bait balls, making prey easier to capture."}],"spanish":"La mandíbula del delfín. Los delfines oyen con la ayuda de sus mandíbulas."},{"name":"Orca tooth","intro":"Orcas grasp and tear prey with strong teeth.","questions":[{"q":"Why do Pacific Northwest orcas visit Monterey Bay?","answer":"Some fish-eating orcas travel in search of prey when food is scarce. Different orca populations have different prey preferences; do not assume all local orcas eat salmon."},{"q":"What whales do orcas hunt in Monterey Bay?","answer":"Some mammal-eating orcas hunt gray whales, especially vulnerable calves during their northward migration; orcas also pursue other marine mammals."}],"spanish":"El diente de una orca. Las orcas usan sus dientes para sujetar y despedazar a su presa."},{"name":"Elephant seal front flipper","intro":"Elephant seal front flippers have five digits, like a human hand.","questions":[{"q":"How many northern elephant seals live in California?","answer":"Once nearly hunted to extinction for their oil, northern elephant seals rebounded following protections. The source handout cites more than 160,000 in one section and more than 200,000 in another; treat these as differing estimates, not one confirmed count."},{"q":"Why is bigger better for male elephant seals?","answer":"Large bulls can defend breeding territories and mate with many females; smaller males are often excluded by dominant bulls."}],"spanish":"La aleta delantera de la foca elefante. Tienen cinco dígitos similares a los de una mano humana."},{"name":"Sea otter skull","intro":"Powerful jaws and teeth help sea otters crush hard-shelled prey.","questions":[{"q":"How do sea otters help kelp forests?","answer":"Sea otters eat sea urchins. By limiting urchin grazing, they help maintain kelp forests and the many species these forests support."},{"q":"What do sea otters keep in their pockets?","answer":"Loose folds of skin beneath the forelimbs can hold food or favorite stones. Sea otters use rocks to break open shellfish."}],"spanish":"Cráneo de nutria marina. Poderosas mandíbulas y dientes permiten que las nutrias marinas sean superdepredadores."},{"name":"Humpback whale food","intro":"Monterey Bay provides abundant prey for visiting humpbacks.","questions":[{"q":"Why do humpbacks travel to Monterey Bay?","answer":"Humpbacks migrate from warmer breeding grounds to productive feeding areas, where they eat schooling fish and other prey."},{"q":"Why do humpbacks blow bubbles underwater?","answer":"Some humpbacks, especially in Alaska, cooperate in bubble-net feeding: bubbles concentrate schooling fish and whales lunge upward to capture them."}],"spanish":"Alimento del rorcual jorobado. La Bahía de Monterey ofrece abundante alimento."}]},{"title":"Sea Slug Search 2026 — volunteer opportunity","text":"The author is volunteering Friday morning with the Pacific Grove Museum of Natural History for Sea Slug Search 2026, hosted by the California Academy of Sciences on iNaturalist. A recent survey at Asilomar found dozens of nudibranchs spanning more than a dozen species. The event announcement is preserved as originally described, not presented as a current upcoming event."},{"title":"Hopkins’ rose — eggs and nudibranch life cycle","text":"Hopkins’ rose lays pink egg masses. The source describes a typical nudibranch life cycle: eggs hatch into swimming veliger larvae; larvae settle, lose their larval shells and develop into benthic juveniles before reaching reproductive maturity. Nudibranchs are simultaneous hermaphrodites. Species-specific development has not been well studied under human care.","photos":[{"src":"IMG_3465.jpeg","caption":"Hopkins’ rose nudibranchs and their pink egg ribbon — iNaturalist."},{"src":"IMG_3466.jpeg","caption":"Nudibranch life cycle — Zoe Lawrence."}]},{"title":"Hopkins’ rose — dorid, not aeolid","text":"Despite its many long papillae, Hopkins’ rose is a dorid nudibranch, not an aeolid. Dorids possess a branchial plume of gills near the anus, whereas aeolids have dorsal cerata. The gill plumes and sensory rhinophores of Hopkins’ rose can be hidden among the papillae. One supplied comparison graphic incorrectly labels Hopkins’ rose as an aeolid; this is noted rather than endorsed.","photos":[{"src":"IMG_3467.jpeg","caption":"Dorid versus aeolid nudibranch anatomy — Kitty Turner."},{"src":"IMG_3468.jpeg","caption":"Dorid versus aeolid comparison; source incorrectly identifies Hopkins’ rose as an aeolid."},{"src":"IMG_3469.jpeg","caption":"Branchial plumes (anal gills) and flat foot — Robin Agarwal."},{"src":"IMG_3470.jpeg","caption":"Rhinophores among the papillae — Jerry Kirkhart."},{"src":"IMG_3471.jpeg","caption":"Full-body Hopkins’ rose, showing rhinophores and gills among pale papillae — iNaturalist."}]},{"title":"Hopkins’ rose — northward range shifts and coloration","text":"The source describes Hopkins’ rose as a warm-water nudibranch increasingly observed northward during marine heatwaves and El Niño periods, including the 2015–16 event and occasional Southern Oregon records. These changes can indicate shifts in ocean conditions. The slug feeds on red bryozoans, including Integripelta bilabiata. Pigments from prey, including hopkinsiaxanthin, contribute to its bright pink coloration. The source discusses warning coloration and chemical defenses; coloration alone should not be treated as a direct measure of health.","links":[{"label":"Monterey Bay Aquarium — Hopkins’ rose","url":"https://www.montereybayaquarium.org/animals-the-ocean/animals-a-to-z/hopkins-rose"},{"label":"California Academy of Sciences — range shifts","url":"https://www.calacademy.org/explore-science/nudibranchs-could-signify-ocean-shift"},{"label":"MolluscaBase — taxonomy","url":"https://www.molluscabase.org/aphia.php?p=taxdetails&id=1723348"},{"label":"Science Friday — Hopkins’ rose","url":"https://www.sciencefriday.com/articles/picture-of-the-week-hopkins-rose-sea-slug/"},{"label":"Marine Sanctuary Foundation — sea wonder","url":"https://marinesanctuary.org/blog/sea-wonder-hopkins-rose-nudibranch/"},{"label":"PubChem — hopkinsiaxanthin","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/20055195"}]}],"highlights":[{"name":"Hopkins’ rose nudibranch","scientific":"Ceratodoris rosacea","intro":"Pink dorid nudibranch whose northward appearances can indicate warmer ocean conditions; see the illustrated life cycle, anatomy, and color discussions in this Field Note.","facts":["Length: approximately 1 inch (2–3 cm).","Diet: red bryozoans, including Integripelta bilabiata.","Predator: California aglaja (Navanax inermis).","Habitat: rocky intertidal; source lists depths of 0–20 ft.","Range: northern Baja California to central California, with warm-period sightings as far north as southern Oregon.","Lifespan: source estimates 1–1.5 years; conservation status: Not Evaluated.","Dorid gill plumes are hidden among papillae; rhinophores detect chemical cues.","Prey-derived pigments contribute to its pink color."],"topics":["Egg ribbons and veliger larvae","Dorid versus aeolid","Gills and rhinophores","Diet and coloration","Range shifts and ocean warming"]}],"qa":[],"bay":["Humpback whales","Orcas (CA51)","Risso’s dolphins","Pacific white-sided dolphins","Northern right whale dolphins","Long-beaked common dolphins","Harbor porpoises","Southern sea otters","California sea lions","Harbor seals","Black-footed albatrosses","Sooty shearwaters","Pink-footed shearwaters","California brown pelicans","Common murres","Pigeon guillemots","Brandt's cormorants","Pelagic cormorants","Western gulls","Heermann's gulls","Caspian tern","Common loons","Pacific loons","Red-throated loons","Red-necked phalaropes","Red phalaropes","Velellas"]},{"id":"2026-10-07-blue-spotted-ray","date":"October 1–7, 2026","title":"Blue-Spotted Ribbontail Ray • Stargazers • Humpbacks • Ocean Discoveries","summary":["Humpback encounters, orca interactions, common dolphins, balloon retrievals and whale entanglement technology.","Michael Gilmartin's stargazer account, plus sunflower star research and five marine science updates.","Species Highlight: blue-spotted ribbontail ray (Taeniura lymma).","Bay Watch October 1–7, 2026; 16 source photographs and illustrations."],"observations":[{"title":"Humpbacks, orcas, dolphins and hydrophone","text":"The shift author reports loud humpback singing on the nighttime MBARI hydrophone; four humpbacks approached and stayed near the boat for about an hour. Other observations included bubbling near the surface, chin hairs with barnacles and whale lice, common dolphin aggregations exceeding 1,000, and orca pods repeatedly entering Monterey Bay and being chased away by humpbacks. ","photos":[{"src":"IMG_3431.jpeg","caption":"One of four friendly humpbacks near the boat"},{"src":"IMG_3432.jpeg","caption":"Three of four humpbacks passing close to the bow"},{"src":"IMG_3433.jpeg","caption":"Humpback blowing bubbles while surfacing"},{"src":"IMG_3434.jpeg","caption":"Humpback chin hairs, barnacles and whale lice"},{"src":"IMG_3435.jpeg","caption":"Multiple common dolphin pods traveling together"},{"src":"IMG_3436.jpeg","caption":"Orcas active in Monterey Bay"}]},{"title":"Balloon retrievals and whale entanglement tracking","text":"The crew retrieved several balloons from the ocean. During a Nature Conservancy charter, staff described recently approved tracking buoys that entanglement-response teams can attach to gear when a whale cannot be freed immediately. The device may help teams relocate the whale or recover remaining ghost gear. This is a report of a discussion, not independent confirmation of approval details.","photos":[{"src":"IMG_3437.jpeg","caption":"Balloon retrieval by the crew"}]},{"title":"Hermit crab shell theft","text":"At the end of a Thursday shift, the author watched one hermit crab forcibly remove another from its shell and occupy the shell. The displaced crab was left without protection."},{"title":"Antarctic krill at a Southern Ocean hydrothermal vent — shared by Candie","text":"A research team led by Kim Bernard observed Antarctic krill, including egg-bearing females, at a hydrothermal vent approximately 3,600 feet deep. Samples suggested feeding on microbes and elevated manganese in tissues; the potential reproductive importance of the vent remains a hypothesis.","links":[{"label":"Research paper: Deep sea hydrothermal vents support gravid Antarctic krill","url":"https://www.nature.com/search?q=Deep%20sea%20hydrothermal%20vents%20support%20gravid%20Antarctic%20krill"}]},{"title":"Dolphin inducing fish regurgitation — shared by Candie","text":"On the Great Barrier Reef, observers documented a bottlenose dolphin nicknamed Bubbles chasing bigeye trevally and eating regurgitated stomach contents. The researchers describe this as apparent kleptoparasitism.","links":[{"label":"Research title: Repeated observations suggesting kleptoparasitism","url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/action/doSearch?AllField=Repeated+observations+suggesting+kleptoparasitism+by+a+Common+Bottlenose+Dolphin"}]},{"title":"Marine litter in Argentina's Mar del Plata Canyon — shared by Candie","text":"A 2025 deep-sea ROV expedition reported 40 potentially undescribed species and 29 human-made objects across ten dives, including fishing debris and plastics. Marine life was observed around some litter.","links":[{"label":"PLOS ONE research search","url":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/search?q=Out%20of%20sight%20hidden%20in%20the%20deep"}]},{"title":"Nine new sponge species — shared by Candie","text":"NOAA Alaska Fisheries Science Center scientists examined trawl-survey specimens and identified nine new sponge species using skeletal characters and genetics. Sponges filter seawater and provide seafloor habitat.","links":[{"label":"NOAA Fisheries","url":"https://www.fisheries.noaa.gov/"}]},{"title":"El Niño monitoring — shared by Candie","text":"The email highlights NOAA's Tropical Pacific Observing System: satellites, moored buoys, drifting instruments, profiling floats, gliders and uncrewed surface vehicles. These help track evolving ENSO conditions. The source email discusses a potential major El Niño; this is a contemporary forecast, not an established outcome.","links":[{"label":"NOAA Fisheries West Coast ENSO indicators","url":"https://www.fisheries.noaa.gov/feature-story/ocean-indicators-were-tracking-el-ninos-arrival-west-coast"},{"label":"NASA JPL Sea Level","url":"https://sealevel.jpl.nasa.gov/"},{"label":"NASA Sentinel-6 and El Niño precursor","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/international-sea-level-satellite-observes-el-nino-precursor/"},{"label":"CeNCOOS","url":"https://www.cencoos.org/"},{"label":"NOAA Climate Prediction Center","url":"https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/"},{"label":"NWS Monterey","url":"https://www.weather.gov/mtr"}],"photos":[{"src":"IMG_3438.jpeg","caption":"NOAA Tropical Pacific Observing System (TPOS)"}]},{"title":"Stargazers (Astroscopus) — contributed by Michael Gilmartin; shared by Sallie","text":"Michael's Friday shift account introduces stargazers added to the tube-anemone tank. Uranoscopidae are sand-burying ambush predators with upward-facing eyes and mouths; they prey on small fish and benthic invertebrates. Some species have lures, venomous spines, and electric organs derived from modified eye muscles. Eggs float and juveniles develop upward-facing eyes as they mature. ","links":[{"label":"Open permanent Stargazer Guide","url":"../stargazer-species-guide/index.html"},{"label":"Stargazer video","url":"https://youtu.be/lRF4dKmByRU"}],"photos":[{"src":"IMG_3439.jpeg","caption":"Stargazer resting on sand"},{"src":"IMG_3440.jpeg","caption":"Camouflaged stargazer eyes and mouth"},{"src":"IMG_3441.jpeg","caption":"Stargazer partly buried in sand"},{"src":"IMG_3442.jpeg","caption":"Juvenile stargazers"}]},{"title":"Sunflower sea stars and purple urchins","text":"The Sunflower Star Laboratory shared a temporary outplanting experiment offering growing sunflower stars either well-fed or starved purple urchins. The stars presented with nutritionally poor urchins ate more of them, suggesting potential usefulness in urchin barrens. The source also reports two stars lost to wasting; interpretation of the cause remains uncertain."},{"title":"Blue-spotted ribbontail ray — species highlight","text":"The week's highlighted species is Taeniura lymma, a colorful Indo-Pacific whiptail stingray associated with coral reefs. The original email includes a distribution map, anatomy artwork by Jacqueline Rajerison, an infographic and microscopy of its structural blue coloration. ","links":[{"label":"Open permanent Ribbontail Ray Guide","url":"../bluespotted-ribbontail-ray/index.html"}],"photos":[{"src":"IMG_3443.jpeg","caption":"Blue-spotted ribbontail ray range"},{"src":"IMG_3444.jpeg","caption":"Ribbontail ray anatomy — artwork by Jacqueline Rajerison"},{"src":"IMG_3445.jpeg","caption":"Blue-spotted ray biology infographic"},{"src":"IMG_3446.jpeg","caption":"Microscopy and illustration of structural blue coloration"}]}],"highlights":[{"name":"Stargazer","scientific":"Uranoscopidae • Astroscopus","url":"../stargazer-species-guide/index.html","intro":"Stargazer account contributed by Michael Gilmartin and shared by Sallie; see original observations and photographs above.","facts":["Eyes and upward-facing mouth are positioned on top of a broad head.","Uses strong pectoral fins to bury itself in sand; specialized breathing openings help keep sediment out.","Ambushes small fish, crustaceans and other bottom-dwelling invertebrates; some species use a worm-like lure.","Venomous spines occur near the gill covers; some Astroscopus species can generate electric discharges using modified eye muscles.","Eggs rise into the water column; planktonic larvae later settle and develop upward-facing eyes.","An Astroscopus stargazer was introduced to the aquarium tube-anemone tank, as described in the shift update."],"topics":["Sand camouflage and breathing","Ambush feeding and lures","Venom and electric organs","Eggs, larvae and development","Visitor interpretation"]}],"highlight":{"name":"Blue-spotted ribbontail ray","scientific":"Taeniura lymma","intro":"Also called bluespotted lagoon ray or bluespotted ribbontail ray. Adults are strongly associated with coral reefs; juveniles use sheltered shallow habitats. The bright blue spots arise from microscopic structures that reflect blue light.","url":"../bluespotted-ribbontail-ray/index.html","facts":["Disc width roughly 12–14 inches; total length up to approximately 28 inches, as reported in the shift email.","Diet: crustaceans, mollusks and polychaete worms; predators include sharks and dolphins.","Indo-Pacific and coastal Indian Ocean; typically shallow waters to approximately 66 feet.","Viviparous; females reportedly bear 2–7 pups following gestation that varies with conditions.","Ampullae of Lorenzini detect prey's weak electrical signals; crushing tooth plates process hard-shelled prey.","Tail bears venomous serrated spines; do not touch or approach closely.","IUCN conservation status reported as Least Concern; lifespan unknown."],"topics":["Life cycle and sexual dimorphism","Reef and nursery habitats","Electroreception","Stinging barbs and anatomy","Structural blue coloration","Cleaner wrasse interactions","Nocturnal feeding"]},"qa":[],"bay":["Humpback whales","Orcas (CA51A with CA50B, CA51 and CA216C pods)","Risso’s dolphins","Long-beaked common dolphins (pods over 1,000)","Bottlenose dolphins","Harbor porpoises","Southern sea otters","California sea lions","Harbor seals","Northern elephant seals (occasional pup at San Carlos)","Pink-footed shearwaters","Black-vented shearwaters","Manx shearwater","California brown pelicans","Common murres","Pigeon guillemots","Cassin’s auklets","Brandt's cormorants","Pelagic cormorants","Western gulls","Heermann's gulls","California gulls","Sabine’s gulls","Common terns","Elegant terns","Parasitic jaegers","Pomarine jaegers","Red-necked phalaropes","Red-breasted mergansers","Common loon","Red-throated loon","Moon jellies"]},{"id":"2026-05-28-elephant-seals","date":"May 28, 2026 (Bay Watch May 21–27)","title":"Northern Elephant Seals • Deep-Sea Sleep • Conservation Comeback","summary":["Northern elephant seal biology, breeding, migration and deep-sea sleep.","Eleven original illustrated graphics, including conservation history.","Marine Mammal Cart operations and wildlife rescue hotline.","Bay Watch: May 21–27, 2026 — 32 sightings groups."],"observations":[{"title":"Shift update and volunteer context","text":"The May 28 shift update included staff scheduling information and praised the mobile-friendly cliff notes on the Marine Mammal Cart and Hopkins’ rose nudibranch. The Marine Mammal Cart is a hard station: give priority to relieving guides moving between hard stations; arrive about five minutes early when moving from a soft station."},{"title":"Northern elephant seal — size and identification","text":"Northern elephant seals (Mirounga angustirostris) are the largest seals in the Northern Hemisphere. Southern elephant seals are larger; walruses rank third among pinnipeds. Males may reach about 13 ft and 4,400 lb; females about 10 ft and 1,300 lb.","photos":[{"src":"../northern-elephant-seal-pwa-v1.2-final-hub%20(2)/images/size_compare.jpg","caption":"Three largest pinnipeds — Peppermint Narwhal Creative."},{"src":"../northern-elephant-seal-pwa-v1.2-final-hub%20(2)/images/size_sketch.jpg","caption":"Male, female and pup size comparison."}]},{"title":"Migration, feeding and diving","text":"Northern elephant seals range across the North Pacific from Alaska to Mexico. Adult males and females use different feeding areas; seals spend most of the year at sea and return to shore to breed and molt. Typical dives reach 1,000–2,000 ft, and a recorded dive reached 5,692 ft. They eat fish, squid and other deep-sea prey; white sharks and orcas are predators.","photos":[{"src":"../northern-elephant-seal-pwa-v1.2-final-hub%20(2)/images/migration_map.jpg","caption":"Male and female migration ranges."}]},{"title":"Breeding calendar, harems and pups","text":"Large males compete for access to females in breeding colonies. A female typically gives birth to one pup, nurses for roughly four weeks on fat-rich milk, and then weans it. Pups can grow from roughly 60–80 lb at birth to 250–350 lb at weaning. Some pups acquire additional milk and become unusually large 'super-weaners'; their later outcomes vary.","photos":[{"src":"../northern-elephant-seal-pwa-v1.2-final-hub%20(2)/images/calendar.jpg","caption":"Annual breeding, molting and haul-out calendar."},{"src":"../northern-elephant-seal-pwa-v1.2-final-hub%20(2)/images/rookery_scene.jpg","caption":"Elephant seal harem and social structure."}]},{"title":"Foraging, body fat and buoyancy","text":"The source contrasts the diet of yearlings with adult feeding patterns. Elephant seals build fat reserves during long foraging trips; changes in body condition can change buoyancy and drifting behavior. The study graphic compares leaner and fatter seals; the graphic should not be interpreted as proving a single cause of survival or mortality.","photos":[{"src":"../northern-elephant-seal-pwa-v1.2-final-hub%20(2)/images/diet_diagram.jpg","caption":"Diet of a yearling northern elephant seal."},{"src":"../northern-elephant-seal-pwa-v1.2-final-hub%20(2)/images/buoyancy_chart.jpg","caption":"Body condition, buoyancy and drift-diving behavior."}]},{"title":"Deep-sea sleep and oxygen management","text":"Northern elephant seals can take brief sleep episodes during deep dives, sometimes around 15 minutes, and may total under two hours of sleep per day at sea, compared with much longer periods on land. Their oxygen stores and diving physiology support prolonged submergence. Unlike dolphins, true seals show bilateral rather than unihemispheric sleep.","photos":[{"src":"../northern-elephant-seal-pwa-v1.2-final-hub%20(2)/images/sleep_diagram.jpg","caption":"Comparison of marine mammal and human sleep patterns."}]},{"title":"Conservation: near-extinction and recovery","text":"Hunting for blubber drove northern elephant seals close to extinction in the nineteenth century; a remnant population survived around Guadalupe Island. Legal protections and reduced hunting allowed a major recovery. The source cites NOAA’s 2024 U.S. stock assessment at more than 200,000 seals; the older infographic shows a smaller historical estimate and should not be treated as the latest population count.","photos":[{"src":"../northern-elephant-seal-pwa-v1.2-final-hub%20(2)/images/cons_basics.jpg","caption":"Conservation history: historical hunting and decline — Peppermint Narwhal Creative."},{"src":"../northern-elephant-seal-pwa-v1.2-final-hub%20(2)/images/cons_comeback.jpg","caption":"Conservation recovery timeline — Peppermint Narwhal Creative."},{"src":"../northern-elephant-seal-pwa-v1.2-final-hub%20(2)/images/cons_success.jpg","caption":"Recovery and continuing threats — Peppermint Narwhal Creative."}]},{"title":"Conservation: ongoing vigilance","text":"Despite their IUCN Least Concern classification, low genetic diversity from the historic bottleneck, entanglement, vessel strikes, interactions with people, disease risks and changing breeding-site conditions warrant continued monitoring. Disease scenarios, including avian influenza, are risks rather than evidence of a current outbreak in this population."},{"title":"Visitor guidance and wildlife rescue","text":"Respect beach closures and posted distance rules, never approach or disturb resting seals, and report stranded or distressed marine mammals to the local rescue network. Monterey County marine mammal hotline: (831) 633-6298."}],"highlight":{"name":"Northern Elephant Seal","scientific":"Mirounga angustirostris","intro":"Largest true seal in the Northern Hemisphere; long-distance migrant, deep diver and a major conservation recovery story.","url":"../../apps/northern-elephant-seal-pwa-v1.2-final-hub%20(2)/index.html","facts":["Adult males may reach about 13 ft and 4,400 lb; females about 10 ft and 1,300 lb.","Dives often reach 1,000–2,000 ft; a recorded maximum is 5,692 ft.","The population recovered dramatically after nineteenth-century hunting.","Conservation status: Least Concern; continued monitoring is important."],"topics":["Size and sexual dimorphism","Migration and diet","Breeding and pup development","Diving and sleep","Conservation recovery"]},"contacts":[{"group":"Monterey County","name":"Marine mammal rescue / distress hotline","phone":"(831) 633-6298","note":"For stranded, injured or distressed marine mammals; do not approach or handle animals."}],"qa":[],"bay":["Humpback whales","Orcas (CA51 & CA51A pods)","Risso’s dolphins","Pacific white-sided dolphins","Northern right whale dolphins","Long-beaked common dolphins","Harbor porpoises","Southern sea otters","California sea lions","Harbor seals","Black-footed albatrosses","Sooty shearwaters","Pink-footed shearwaters","California brown pelicans","Common murres","Pigeon guillemots","Brandt's cormorants","Pelagic cormorants","Western gulls","Heermann's gulls","Caspian tern","Common loons","Pacific loons","Red-throated loons","Velellas","Egg yolk jellies","Purple striped jellies","Moon jellies","Twin-sailed salps","Cyclosalpas","Northern comb jellies","Various hydrozoans including siphonophores"]},{"id":"2026-05-28-humpback-carcass","date":"May 28–June 3, 2026","title":"Sunflower Star Exhibit • Deceased Humpback at Hopkins • Marine Mammal Cart • TCLs • Whale Falls","summary":["New sunflower star exhibit replaces the former spider crab display near the GPO tank.","A deceased juvenile humpback drifted into the Hopkins harbor seal cove; cause of death and identity were not established.","Marine Mammal Cart hard-station priorities and Teen Conservation Leader safety guidance.","Bay Watch: 29 reported entries; no photographs supplied."],"observations":[{"title":"Source dating and attribution","text":"Bay Watch covers May 28–June 3, 2026, as confirmed by the user. The exact email date is not provided. The source also refers to a Thursday the 2nd and June 8–August 3 for TCLs; these scheduling statements are preserved as written."},{"title":"Marine Mammal Cart — hard station","text":"The Marine Mammal Cart is launching as a hard station because of sensitive technology and props. Hard stations take priority over soft stations. Guides moving from one hard station to another should be relieved first. Consult or photograph the master schedule or ask other guides if unsure. Guides at soft stations should arrive at hard stations approximately five minutes early so colleagues can move to their next assignment."},{"title":"Teen Conservation Leaders — welcome and safeguarding","text":"The email says TCLs arrive Thursday the 2nd and that the volunteer lounge is nut-free for snacks June 8–August 3 because of allergies. When uncertain about a snack, do not bring it. TCLs are minors, always paired with guides and not assigned independent lines. Guides who prefer not to be paired should notify shift captains. Do not request personal contact information or meet privately one-on-one; conversations must occur in public or with another volunteer/staff member present. Concerns may be raised with TCL, IP, or VE staff. Welcome new participants without pressuring them to open up."},{"title":"Sunflower stars return to public display","text":"Exhibits staff converted the former spider crab/decorator crab display to a sunflower star exhibit, to the left of the GPO tank before entering MBH. Sunflower stars had been behind the scenes for well over a year. Exhibit staff consulted Sunflower Star Laboratory colleagues about tank conditions, biology and accurate bronze models. The email reports the exhibit is already installed; additional staff interpretation was anticipated."},{"title":"Activity on Monterey Bay","text":"Scattered humpback whales over the canyon, a few blue whales, black-footed albatross sightings, active dolphins and several passing orca pods were reported. Egg yolk jellies were especially abundant and appeared to be eaten by ocean sunfish (molas). Other jellyfish were also reported."},{"title":"Deceased humpback at Hopkins — observation, not diagnosis","text":"Whale-watching boats first spotted a dead humpback on Saturday. It later floated past Pacific Grove and drifted into Hopkins harbor seal beach cove, where it became trapped against rocks. The whale was belly-up and noticeably more swollen on a later sighting. The observer described a juvenile nearing maturity and a seemingly mostly white submerged tail, but no official identity or cause of death was available. Small round wounds were visible, with their origin uncertain. No obvious gear or residual entanglement injuries were visible, and the whale did not initially look underweight; these observations do not exclude any cause. Access for investigation was difficult."},{"title":"Harbor seals and potential predators","text":"Harbor seals appeared to leave their usual cove after the carcass arrived. A few young seals investigated and departed. The observer noted that the carcass could draw scavenging sharks, including large white sharks; nearby beach closures were described as precautionary. A salmon shark seen off Marina Beach may have been coincidental. The email judged orca scavenging less likely given visual access and cetaceans' lack of conventional smell; this was an interpretation, not a confirmed prediction."},{"title":"Whale-fall ecology and carcass management","text":"Decomposition gases can swell whale carcasses, but dramatic explosions are not typical or inevitable. Towing a carcass from this rocky, restricted cove could be hazardous, especially in forecast high winds. A decomposing whale can transfer nutrients and energy to beach, reef, seafloor or deep-sea ecosystems; this can temporarily change local ecological dynamics."},{"title":"Threats to humpback whales","text":"Entanglement and vessel strikes were identified as major threats, alongside predation, climate-driven food-web changes, ocean warming and acidification, and exposure to persistent contaminants such as PFAS. The email cites an international claim that a 10% ship-speed reduction was associated with 50% fewer whale collisions and 40% less ship noise; this numerical claim is retained as source attribution, not independently verified."},{"title":"Visitor conversations and emotional boundaries","text":"Guides uncomfortable discussing the dead humpback may politely decline, redirect to another topic or refer guests to another willing guide. The author offered to help answer questions. The source emphasizes avoiding speculation and respecting individual comfort levels."},{"title":"Related guide mentioned","text":"The email mentions a Red abalone guide created from previous shift emails. No destination URL was supplied, so no link has been invented."}],"highlight":null,"qa":[{"q":"How did the whale die?","short":"No official cause of death had been determined. The belly-up position obscured the back, so possible ship-strike injuries could not be assessed. Small round wounds had uncertain origins; no obvious fishing gear or entanglement scars were seen, and the whale did not initially appear underweight. These observations cannot establish or exclude a cause. Do not speculate with guests.","tags":["humpback","mortality","uncertainty"]},{"q":"What will happen to the body? Will it explode?","short":"The outcome depends on where the carcass ends up and how decomposition proceeds. Gases can cause swelling, but postmortem openings and scavenging can release pressure; explosive rupture is not inevitable and is uncommon. This individual was reported to be increasingly swollen.","tags":["carcass","decomposition"]},{"q":"Will the body attract sharks or orcas?","short":"Scavenging sharks may be attracted to the carcass, potentially including mature white sharks. Nearby beach closures were described as precautionary. A salmon shark sighting may have been unrelated. The author thought orca scavenging was less likely because the carcass was hard to see behind kelp and cetaceans lack conventional olfaction; this remains an interpretation.","tags":["sharks","orcas","safety"]},{"q":"Do we know which whale it was?","short":"No official individual identification was reported. The mostly submerged tail appeared unusually white, but a tail photograph or confirmed match was unavailable. The author had a possible identification in mind but did not claim it as fact.","tags":["humpback","identification"]},{"q":"What will happen to the harbor seals?","short":"The seals appeared to avoid their normal beach while the carcass occupied the cove. Some weaned pups briefly investigated. Whether or when the seals return was unknown and may depend on where the body ends up, its decay and predator activity.","tags":["harbor seals","behavior"]},{"q":"Why don't they tow the body out to sea?","short":"Agencies sometimes relocate whale carcasses, but the Hopkins cove is rocky and difficult to reach safely by boat; forecast winds would add risk. The site is not intended for tourist access. Leaving the carcass can also support natural nutrient recycling, although the decision and ultimate outcome were not confirmed.","tags":["whale fall","management"]},{"q":"What threats do humpback whales face?","short":"Major threats discussed were entanglement and vessel strikes, plus changing prey distributions and food webs, warming and acidification, PFAS exposure, and predation. The author highlighted vessel-speed reductions as protective; quoted global speed-reduction statistics were not independently checked.","tags":["humpback","conservation","ship strikes"]},{"q":"What if I don't feel comfortable discussing this with guests?","short":"You may politely say you would rather not discuss the topic and refer the guest to another willing guide or change subjects. The email's author offered to help guides with difficult conversations.","tags":["visitor communication","wellbeing"]}],"bay":["Humpback whales","Orcas (CA51s, CA35s, CA36, CA140s, CA216s, CA222s, CA175A, CA180 pods/orcas)","Risso’s dolphins","Pacific white-sided dolphins","Northern right whale dolphins","Long-beaked common dolphins","Harbor porpoises","Southern sea otters","California sea lions","Harbor seals","Black-footed albatrosses","Sooty shearwaters","Pink-footed shearwaters","California brown pelicans","Common murres","Pigeon guillemots","Brandt's cormorants","Pelagic cormorants","Western gulls","Heermann's gulls","Caspian tern","Common loons","Velellas","Egg yolk jellies","Purple striped jellies","Moon jellies","Salmon shark","South polar skua","Horned puffin"]},{"id":"2026-06-08-stranding","date":"June 8, 2026 (photographs June 3–4)","title":"Two stranded humpbacks, necropsies & domoic acid questions","summary":["Hopkins stranded humpback was confirmed male and estimated 2–3 years old; samples were collected during necropsy","A second young humpback stranded at Sunset Beach; identification and cause of death were not confirmed in the source","Domoic acid exposure was a possibility under investigation, not an established cause of death","Three original Glenn A. Takenaga photographs dated June 3 and 4, 2026"],"observations":[{"title":"Hopkins stranding — necropsy and identification","text":"MJ reports that a necropsy was performed on the humpback at Hopkins and blubber, fecal, baleen and bone samples were taken; whether an earwax plug was collected was unknown. The whale was confirmed male despite postmortem genital swelling that initially suggested a female. Samples suggested an age of about 2–3 years; he appeared large for his age. His mother was unknown, and maternal size, genetics, early nutrition and diet were suggested as possible explanations. The source expected an attempt to tow the whale offshore, potentially into Monterey Canyon, but did not confirm the outcome.","photos":[{"src":"IMG_3400.jpeg","caption":"Hopkins stranded humpback near rocky shoreline, June 3, 2026. Photograph: Glenn A. Takenaga."},{"src":"IMG_3401.jpeg","caption":"Hopkins stranded humpback on beach, wide view, June 4, 2026. Photograph: Glenn A. Takenaga."},{"src":"IMG_3402.jpeg","caption":"Hopkins stranded humpback near rocks, June 4, 2026. Photograph: Glenn A. Takenaga. This image matches the June 3 photograph supplied; retained as separately dated submission."}]},{"title":"Sunset Beach — second young humpback","text":"A second young humpback washed ashore at Sunset Beach. MJ had not heard whether this animal had been identified. Necropsy and towing were anticipated, but results and disposition were not provided."},{"title":"Domoic acid — suspected, not confirmed","text":"MJ reports that investigators were considering domoic acid exposure and testing fecal samples. Pseudo-nitzschia had been detected locally without a strong local domoic acid signal reported at the time. A substantial bloom in Southern California was noted as a potential exposure source during whale migration. Filter-feeding anchovies can accumulate domoic acid, which can then reach predators. Effects in juvenile humpbacks remain uncertain; no cause of death was established for either whale in the supplied email."},{"title":"Harmful algal blooms and broader context","text":"The source describes domoic acid as a neurotoxin capable of harming birds and mammals, discusses possible differences in exposure and susceptibility between juvenile and adult baleen whales, and references the large sei whale mortality event in Chile in 2015–2016. MJ emphasized limited evidence on effects in large baleen whales and the need to avoid assuming domoic acid caused these strandings."},{"title":"Live monitoring resources","text":"MJ provided three resources for checking domoic acid, harmful algal blooms and water conditions.","links":[{"label":"California Domoic Acid Tracking","url":"https://sccoos-ddt.shinyapps.io/california-da-tracking/"},{"label":"California HAB Map — Monterey Wharf","url":"https://data.caloos.org/#dashboards/layer/59cb173d-9fab-44d0-9a13-5e1c35a10f1b/location_name?location_name=Monterey%20Wharf"},{"label":"Aquarium seawater intake station data","url":"https://data.caloos.org/#metadata/103387/station/data"}]},{"title":"Public communication and misinformation","text":"MJ described speaking to local news to clarify differences between recent gray whale and humpback whale strandings and to address misinformation. Interview linked below.","links":[{"label":"Watch MJ’s interview on YouTube","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4Y__xzw_rps"}]}],"qa":[],"bay":[],"resources":["California Domoic Acid Tracking","California HAB Map","Aquarium seawater intake station data"]},{"id":"2026-06-18-24","date":"June 18–24, 2026","title":"Whale parasites, vessel strikes & returning humpbacks","summary":["Blue and fin whales departed quickly as familiar humpbacks returned","Preliminary necropsy findings from an Alaska fin whale vessel strike and regional conservation concerns","Parasites Highlight: cookiecutter sharks, whalesuckers, whale barnacles and whale lice","Eight supplied illustrated images, including two 'Where are the boops?' challenges"],"observations":[{"title":"Whale activity and familiar humpbacks","text":"Blue and fin whales were plentiful Monday but had largely departed by Tuesday, when only sparse humpbacks, a few dolphin pods and one lone male orca were found. Humpbacks later returned, including Snowy Owl, Aurora, Ratchet and Fingers."},{"title":"Alaska fin whale vessel strike and conservation","text":"The source reports preliminary necropsy findings that the pregnant fin whale was healthy, had a good blubber layer and died from blunt trauma associated with a vessel strike. It describes fin whales as 20% of known whales involved in vessel strikes; discusses regional vessel-speed and distance protections, Monterey cruise-ship restrictions, planned crab-trap restrictions in CDFW zones 4 and 5, and renewed Icelandic fin whaling. These are historical statements from the email, not independently verified current policies."},{"title":"Cookiecutter shark bites — Isistius brasiliensis","text":"Smalltooth cookiecutter sharks leave round bite wounds and scars on whales, sea lions, dolphins, other sharks, rays and fish. The source connects summertime marks on blue and fin whales with offshore feeding and nighttime vertical migration.","photos":[{"src":"../whale-associated-organisms/IMG_3392.jpeg","caption":"Fin whale displaying scars and skin damage from remoras and cookiecutter shark bites."}]},{"title":"Whalesuckers — Remora australis","text":"Whalesuckers attach with a modified dorsal-fin adhesive disk. The source describes their relationship with whales as often mutualistic or relatively harmless, although prolonged attachment can cause skin damage. Examples include blue whales, Risso’s dolphins and long-beaked common dolphins.","photos":[{"src":"../whale-associated-organisms/IMG_3393.jpeg","caption":"Blue whale with a single black whalesucker on the dorsal fin; below, a blue whale with many whalesuckers just below the dorsal fin."},{"src":"../whale-associated-organisms/IMG_3394.jpeg","caption":"Risso’s dolphin with a faint whalesucker scar; below, a long-beaked common dolphin with a whalesucker scar."}]},{"title":"Whale barnacles and rabbit-ear gooseneck barnacles","text":"The source contrasts Coronula reginae, found on multiple baleen whale species, with Coronula diadema, most associated with humpbacks, and Cryptolepas rhachianecti on gray whales. Rabbit-ear barnacles (Conchoderma auritum) can grow on whale barnacles. Breaching may dislodge attached barnacles.","photos":[{"src":"../whale-associated-organisms/IMG_3395.jpeg","caption":"Breaching humpback calf coated in whale barnacles and gooseneck barnacles."},{"src":"../whale-associated-organisms/IMG_3396.jpeg","caption":"Healthy Coronula diadema barnacles growing along a humpback whale’s chin."},{"src":"../whale-associated-organisms/IMG_3397.jpeg","caption":"Gray whale with Cryptolepas rhachianecti barnacles; lower annotated image circles barnacle clusters."}]},{"title":"Whale lice — Cyamus boopis: two photo challenges","text":"Whale lice are crustaceans rather than insects. The source describes them grazing on algae and dead skin, remaining on whale hosts throughout their life cycles and transferring when whales come close. Challenge 1 and challenge 2 each preserve the original unmarked view followed by the marked answer in the same supplied image.","photos":[{"src":"../whale-associated-organisms/IMG_3398.jpeg","caption":"Whale lice challenge 1: Where are the boops? Below, the annotated answer."},{"src":"../whale-associated-organisms/IMG_3399.jpeg","caption":"Whale lice challenge 2: Where are the boops? Below, the annotated answer."}]},{"title":"Whales as mobile ecosystems","text":"Whales provide transport, shelter and feeding opportunities for associated organisms. Many external associates cause little harm, and some are beneficial. The source notes that coastal whales often have more external associates than offshore whales."},{"title":"Shared articles","text":"The source shared articles on a humpback crossing the Arabian Sea and sperm whale dialects in the Mediterranean.","links":[{"label":"Humpback whale crossing the Arabian Sea — Popular Science","url":"https://www.popsci.com/environment/humpback-whale-cross-arabian-sea/"},{"label":"Mediterranean whale dialects — ScienceAlert","url":"https://www.sciencealert.com/whales-appear-to-be-evolving-a-new-dialect-in-the-mediterranean-sea"}]}],"highlight":{"name":"Whale-associated organisms","scientific":"Isistius brasiliensis · Remora australis · Coronula spp. · Cryptolepas rhachianecti · Conchoderma auritum · Cyamus boopis","url":"../whale-associated-organisms/index.html","intro":"A guide to whale-associated organisms, distinguishing parasitism, commensalism and mutualism.","facts":["Cookiecutter sharks leave circular bite scars","Whalesuckers attach by modified dorsal-fin disks","Whale barnacles attach to whale skin and can host other barnacles","Whale lice are crustaceans, not insects"],"topics":["Cookiecutter sharks","Whalesuckers","Whale barnacles","Whale lice","Whales as mobile ecosystems"]},"qa":[],"bay":["Humpback whales","Blue whales","Fin whales","Minke whales","Sperm whales","Orca (CA199, solitary male)","Risso’s dolphins","Pacific white-sided dolphins","Northern right whale dolphins","Long-beaked common dolphins","Harbor porpoises","Southern sea otters","California sea lions","Harbor seals","Black-footed albatrosses","Sooty shearwaters","Pink-footed shearwaters","Northern fulmar","California brown pelicans","Common murres","Pigeon guillemots","Brandt's cormorants","Pelagic cormorants","Western gulls","Heermann's gulls","Caspian tern","Ocean sunfishes","Egg yolk jellies","Twin-sailed salps","Salmon shark","Blue shark"],"resources":["Current Signup Genius","New Signup Genius","Shift Snack Signup","Naturalist Library","How to Log Into the Naturalist Library","Tidepool","How to Log Into Tidepool","MBARI Hydrophone"]},{id:"2026-07-02-08",date:"July 2–8, 2026",title:"Flying fish, whale chin hairs & uncommon storm-petrels",summary:["Fork-tailed and black storm-petrels were seen around Monterey Bay, adding uncommon pelagic birds to the week’s sightings","A first flying-fish sighting included a fish gliding about 100 feet across two swells","A close humpback encounter showed tiny chin hairs, whale barnacles and a whale louse","Species Highlight: California flying fish / Bennett’s flying fish and the adaptations that make gliding possible"],observations:[{title:"Uncommon storm-petrels and a first flying fish",text:"A fork-tailed storm-petrel was blown into the harbor after strong winds, an uncommon offshore bird described in the source as typically seen locally in spring and early summer. Black storm-petrels were also around. The update also recorded a first flying-fish sighting: the fish leapt beside the boat and glided about 100 feet across two swells before splashing back down."},{title:"Humpback chin hairs and a whale louse",text:"A close whale mugging produced a view of a humpback’s chin. Tiny chin hairs are visible among the whale barnacles, and a whale louse can be seen between the barnacles.",photos:[{src:"IMG_3379.jpeg",caption:"Humpback chin. Red circles identify some of the most visible chin hairs; the purple circle identifies a whale louse between whale barnacles."}]},{title:"Cephalopod diversity and changing oceans",text:"The source update shared a Nautilus op-ed about changes in cephalopod diversity and abundance through geological time and how modern ocean changes may be affecting the group.",links:[{label:"The Cephalopods Are Coming — Nautilus",url:"https://nautil.us/the-cephalopods-are-coming-1281395"}]}],highlight:{name:"California flying fish",scientific:"Cheilopogon pinnatibarbatus californicus",url:"../california-flying-fish/index.html",intro:"The source describes California flying fish as the California subspecies of Bennett’s flying fish and an uncommon warm-water visitor whose sightings may increase during marine heatwaves and El Niño events.",facts:["Maximum length: mixed source claims of 16–19 inches","Diet: zooplankton","Predators: pinnipeds, dolphins, seabirds, larger fish and large squid","Habitat: nearshore open water; eggs laid along rocky shores near kelp forests","Depth: typically 0–35 ft and not deeper than the photic zone","Range: tropical and subtropical regions globally, extending into temperate regions when waters are warm","Lifespan: estimated up to 5 years; source notes it is currently unknown","Conservation status: Least Concern (IUCN)"],topics:["Life cycle and egg attachment","Modified pectoral fins and gliding","Predator evasion","Taxiing and stages of gliding","Possible fin-development gene changes","Specialized eyes","Fused vertebrae and skeletal reinforcement","Fisheries and conservation"],photos:[{src:"../california-flying-fish/IMG_3380.jpeg",caption:"A generic body plan for the Cheilopogon fishes."},{src:"../california-flying-fish/IMG_3381.jpeg",caption:"How flying fish glide using speed, fins and tail-assisted taxiing."},{src:"../california-flying-fish/IMG_3382.jpeg",caption:"Infographic on a possible gene change in flying fishes that alters fin proportions associated with gliding."},{src:"../california-flying-fish/IMG_3383.jpeg",caption:"Stages of gliding for flying fish: swimming, leaping and fin deployment, taxiing, gliding and water re-entry."}]},qa:[],bay:["Humpback whales","Fin whales","Minke whales","Orca (CA122B, CA122D, CA212B, CA51A with CA50B, CA220; CA49B seen with other pods)","Risso’s dolphins","Pacific white-sided dolphins","Northern right whale dolphins","Long-beaked common dolphins","Harbor porpoises","Dall’s porpoises","Southern sea otters","California sea lions","Harbor seals","Black-footed albatrosses","Sooty shearwaters","Pink-footed shearwaters","Northern fulmar","Black storm-petrel","Fork-tailed storm-petrel","California brown pelicans","Common murres","Pigeon guillemots","Brandt's cormorants","Pelagic cormorants","Western gulls","Heermann's gulls","Caspian terns","Ocean sunfish","Salmon sharks","Twin-sailed salps","Northern anchovies (large schools splashing at the surface)","Chinook salmon (leaping out of the water)","Flying fish"],resources:["Current Signup Genius","Shift Snack Signup","Naturalist Library","How to Log Into the Naturalist Library","Tidepool","How to Log Into Tidepool","MBARI Hydrophone"]},{id:"2026-07-09-15",date:"July 9–15, 2026",title:"Dolphin feeding frenzy, baby birds & Pseudo-nitzschia",summary:["Hundreds to more than a thousand common dolphins were gathering in Monterey Bay, with smaller pods sometimes passing the aquarium","Baby western gulls and nesting Brandt’s and pelagic cormorants were visible around the aquarium","Species Highlight: Pseudo-nitzschia, domoic acid and harmful algal blooms","Wildlife-rescue warning signs and important local contact numbers were highlighted"],observations:[{title:"Dolphins and seabirds feeding together",text:"Large groups of several hundred to more than a thousand dolphins were being found most mornings. The groups often broke into smaller pods during the day, and some passed the aquarium. On Tuesday afternoon, more than a hundred dolphins were observed herding fish to the surface while pelicans plunge-dived and gulls grabbed fish near the surface. When conditions are good, guides can encourage visitors to look outside for visible activity."},{title:"Babies around the aquarium",text:"Many young birds were visible around the aquarium, including western gull chicks at different stages of development. In addition to pelagic cormorants nesting beneath the aquarium, a Brandt’s cormorant was nesting there. Brandt’s can be distinguished by a thicker bill, yellow chin and a blue throat patch; pelagic cormorants have a more slender bill, red facial skin around the eye and chin, and can show stronger metallic green, blue and purple tones in good light.",photos:[{src:"IMG_3355.jpeg",caption:"Brandt’s cormorant nesting at the aquarium; the female’s blue throat patch is faintly visible."},{src:"IMG_3356.jpeg",caption:"Western gull chick."}]},{title:"Pseudo-nitzschia and harmful algal blooms",text:"Pseudo-nitzschia is a genus of marine diatoms. P. australis and P. multiseries are among the species capable of producing domoic acid and causing harmful algal blooms along the California coast. The presence of Pseudo-nitzschia alone does not mean conditions are dangerous; risk depends on abundance, species, toxin production and environmental conditions. Nutrient-rich upwelling can contribute to blooms, and domoic acid can move through the food web as plankton are eaten by fish, shellfish and other animals.",photos:[{src:"IMG_3357.jpeg",caption:"Typical reproductive phases of Pseudo-nitzschia, including asexual and sexual reproduction."},{src:"IMG_3358.jpeg",caption:"Timeline and West Coast distribution associated with the persistent 2015 Pseudo-nitzschia harmful algal bloom and fisheries closures."}]},{title:"Domoic acid through the food web",text:"Domoic acid can bioaccumulate in prey and expose seabirds and marine mammals that consume contaminated fish, squid or crustaceans. Neurological signs can include disorientation, abnormal behavior, seizures and, in severe cases, death. Domoic acid is heat-stable, so cooking does not reliably destroy it. In people, the recognized syndrome is amnesic shellfish poisoning (ASP), not paralytic shellfish poisoning.",photos:[{src:"IMG_3359.jpeg",caption:"How domoic acid affects glutamate receptors and can produce neurological symptoms."},{src:"IMG_3360.jpeg",caption:"Harmful algal blooms and the food web: Pseudo-nitzschia can pass domoic acid through prey to wildlife and people."}]},{title:"California sea lions and fetal exposure",text:"Research on California sea lions has raised concern about prenatal exposure as well as direct dietary exposure. Domoic acid can reach developing fetuses, and developmental exposure has been associated with neurological effects that may persist later in life. This makes recurring harmful algal blooms important not only for acute strandings but also for longer-term population health.",photos:[{src:"IMG_3361.jpeg",caption:"Overlap between adult female California sea lion range, breeding areas and the general extent of Pseudo-nitzschia blooms."},{src:"IMG_3362.jpeg",caption:"Domoic acid exposure during pregnancy and possible long-term developmental effects in California sea lions."}]},{title:"Wildlife warning signs",text:"Seabirds affected by illness or neurological toxins may appear confused, unusually passive or aggressive, show little fear of people, or wander into inappropriate places. Sea lions with suspected domoic-acid toxicosis may drool or foam, sway the head, have seizures, or hold the head abnormally upward or backward ('stargazing'). Keep people and dogs away from stranded marine mammals and never attempt to handle a sea lion.",photos:[{src:"IMG_3363.jpeg",caption:"Southern California public-alert poster illustrating neurological signs of domoic acid toxicosis. For Monterey Bay response, use the local contacts listed below."}]}],highlight:{name:"Pseudo-nitzschia",scientific:"Pseudo-nitzschia spp.",intro:"A genus of needle-shaped marine diatoms that includes species capable of producing the neurotoxin domoic acid. Blooms can affect food webs, fisheries, seabirds, marine mammals and human seafood safety.",facts:["62 species were reported by the source update as recognized at the time, with 28 known to produce domoic acid","P. australis is an important domoic-acid producer along the California coast","These diatoms occur in marine and brackish waters and are especially relevant in nutrient-rich coastal waters","Domoic acid can move through food webs and become concentrated in prey","In humans, domoic-acid illness is called amnesic shellfish poisoning (ASP)"],topics:["Harmful algal blooms","Domoic acid","Upwelling and nutrients","Food-web bioaccumulation","Wildlife strandings","Human seafood safety","Sea-lion fetal exposure"]},contacts:[{group:"Seabirds / injured birds",name:"SPCA Wildlife Rehabilitation Center",phone:"831-264-5427",note:"Wildlife rehabilitation; summer hours noted in this update: 7 AM–7 PM",address:"1002 Monterey-Salinas Highway, Salinas, CA"},{group:"Sea lions & other marine mammals",name:"The Marine Mammal Center — Central/North Coast hotline",phone:"415-289-7325",note:"24/7 hotline"},{group:"Sea lions & other marine mammals",name:"The Marine Mammal Center — Castroville",phone:"831-633-6298",note:"Business-hours contact"},{group:"Sea otters",name:"Monterey Bay Aquarium",phone:"831-648-4840",note:"Abandoned pups and injured/dead sea otters"}],qa:[{q:"Is seeing Pseudo-nitzschia automatically dangerous?",short:"No. Pseudo-nitzschia can be present at low concentrations without causing a harmful event. Concern rises when toxin-producing species bloom and domoic acid enters the food web.",tags:["Pseudo-nitzschia","HAB","domoic acid"]},{q:"What should I do if I see a sick sea lion?",short:"Keep people and pets at a safe distance, do not approach or handle the animal, and contact the appropriate marine-mammal rescue organization. Photos may be useful if requested by rescuers, but take them only from a safe distance.",tags:["sea lion","wildlife rescue","domoic acid"]}],bay:["Humpback whales","Blue whales","Risso’s dolphins","Long-beaked common dolphins","Harbor porpoises","Southern sea otters","California sea lions","Harbor seals","Black-footed albatrosses","Sooty shearwaters","Pink-footed shearwaters","Black-vented shearwaters","Northern fulmar","Black storm-petrel","California brown pelicans","Common murres","Pigeon guillemots","Brandt's cormorants","Pelagic cormorants","Western gulls","Heermann's gulls","Caspian terns","Red-necked phalarope","Northern osprey (they are back!)","Ocean sunfish","Twin-sailed salps","Purple-striped jellies","Egg yolk jellies","Northern anchovies","Flying fish","Great white sharks (Shark Park is busy with juveniles)"],resources:["Current Signup Genius","Shift Snack Signup","Naturalist Library","How to Log Into the Naturalist Library","Tidepool","How to Log Into Tidepool","MBARI Hydrophone"]},{id:"2026-07-30-08-05",date:"July 30–Aug 5, 2026",title:"Macroalgae safety, abalone aquaculture & a special Bay guest",summary:["Macroalgae toxicity to people is exceptionally rare; environmental contamination is different from toxins naturally produced by algae","Toxic species are more characteristically associated with microalgae or phytoplankton, with Pseudo-nitzschia given as an example","An abalone-farm visit highlighted growing interpretation and outreach plus sea urchins harvested from barrens as sustainable seafood","Bay Watch included a blue whale, huge flocks of sooty shearwaters, fork-tailed petrel, purple-striped jellies and twin-sailed salps"],observations:[{title:"Can macroalgae be toxic?",text:"A question from the previous shift asked whether macroalgae—seaweeds and kelps—can be toxic. The update explains that toxicity to people is exceptionally rare. Macroalgae can produce compounds harmful to other living organisms such as corals, and they can absorb heavy metals or become coated in environmental toxins that could make them harmful to consume, but those contaminants are not substances the macroalgae naturally produce."},{title:"Macroalgae versus toxic microalgae",text:"In general, microalgae or phytoplankton are the group that includes toxic species, and only a small fraction of known species produce toxic substances. Pseudo-nitzschia was given as an example. Macroalgae are generally harmless to people except under specific circumstances. In the context of questions about edible seaweeds and kelps, the source says they are technically safe to eat, although they are not necessarily all tasty."},{title:"Abalone farm visit and sustainable seafood",text:"During tours associated with the abalone festival, the farm was developing an interpretive space about abalone and the sea urchins it harvests as sustainable seafood, particularly urchins taken from barrens. An employee said the operation hoped to expand outreach and education and potentially offer more tours in the future. The visit also provided a look at the farm’s operation beneath the wharf."}],highlight:null,qa:[{q:"Can seaweeds and kelps be toxic to people?",short:"The source says it is exceptionally rare for macroalgae to be toxic to people. They can absorb heavy metals or become coated in environmental toxins, but those contaminants are not toxins the macroalgae naturally produce.",tags:["macroalgae","seaweed","kelp","toxicity"]},{q:"Are all seaweeds and kelps edible?",short:"In the context of this Field Note, the source says they are technically safe to eat, although they are not necessarily all tasty. Specific environmental contamination can still make macroalgae harmful to consume.",tags:["macroalgae","seaweed","kelp","edibility"]}],bay:["Humpback whales","Blue whale!","Minke whales","Risso’s dolphins","Long-beaked common dolphins","Southern sea otters","California sea lions","Harbor seals","Black-footed albatrosses","Sooty shearwaters (huge flocks of thousands; further than the observer could see on the water)","Pink-footed shearwaters","Northern fulmar","Fork-tailed petrel","California brown pelicans","Common murres","Pigeon guillemots","Brandt's cormorants","Pelagic cormorants","Western gulls","Heermann's gulls","Elegant terns","Parasitic jaeger","Red-necked phalaropes","Northern anchovies","Ocean sunfish","Purple-striped jellies","Twin-sailed salps"],resources:[]},{id:"2026-08-06-12",date:"Aug 6–12, 2026",title:"Exhibit ledge ecology, rare seabirds & hydrophone mysteries",summary:["Exhibit ledges highlighted as overlooked microhabitats rich in volunteer organisms from Monterey Bay","Hydrophone recordings included orcas and gunshot-like sounds that could possibly be a North Pacific right whale","Blue whales continued to appear as humpback peak season approached","Rare and unusual birds included Buller’s and Manx shearwaters plus an unidentified masked-or-Nazca booby"],observations:[
{title:"Car Week access reminder",text:"Volunteer Enrichment reported that the circle in front of the volunteer lounge would be barricaded through Aug. 16 to control traffic during Car Week. Volunteers whose drop-off or pick-up is affected were asked to plan accordingly."},
{title:"Hydrophone: orcas and possible right-whale sounds",text:"The hydrophone captured orcas on Tuesday night, described humorously as sounding like melancholy chicken crows. Gunshot-like sounds were also heard that could possibly have been a North Pacific right whale. The update did not confirm the identification. It noted that blue whales had been appearing and suggested krill could be present, which is also a food source for right whales."},
{title:"Seas the Summer in Moss Landing",text:"Four weekends remained in the Seas the Summer program. The Western Flyer Foundation planned Saturday activities such as seaweed pressing or cyanotyping and Sunday tours of the Western Flyer. A pop-up market featured local artists, Sunflower Star Laboratory offered tours, and nature or education books were being collected for a community giveaway table."},
{title:"Approaching humpback peak season and unusual seabirds",text:"Late September to mid-October is usually peak humpback season, although timing varies with conditions and migration. The update also reported more southern-origin birds: an unidentified booby that could have been masked or Nazca, Buller’s shearwaters that breed in New Zealand and range around the Pacific after fledging, and a Manx shearwater—common in the Atlantic but rare in the Pacific—feeding among sooty and pink-footed shearwaters."},
{title:"Mollusk class resources",text:"With advanced mollusk class approaching, guides were reminded to use Tidepool—especially its training materials—and the Naturalist Library sections for Mollusca, Gastropoda, Bivalves, Chitons and Cephalopoda."},
{title:"Exhibit Ledge Ecology — an overlooked microhabitat",text:"Exhibit ledges can support their own microhabitats, with encrusting coralline algae, small red and brown kelps, bryozoans, miniature anemones, top snails, limpets, chitons, sea slugs and other organisms. Larger animals may also use the ledges. Many organisms likely enter through the seawater intake as eggs or larvae, making some appearances seasonal and fleeting while anemones, bivalves, tegulas and limpets can be reliable long-term residents.",photos:[{src:"IMG_2867.jpeg",caption:"An opalescent nudibranch on the ledge inside of the main Monterey Bay Habitats exhibit, taken with my crappy Motorola"},{src:"IMG_2868.jpeg",caption:"I’m sorry, you wanted to look at a sevengill shark? Best I can do is one slug foot."},{src:"IMG_2869.jpeg",caption:"A nudibranch’s egg ribbon, likely from an opalescent nudibranch"},{src:"IMG_2870.jpeg",caption:"A chiton possibly grazing on the ledge of the Kelp Forest exhibit"},{src:"IMG_2871.jpeg",caption:"So sorry for this terrible picture but I swear there is a tiny skeleton shrimp party in the green algae"},{src:"IMG_2873.jpeg",caption:"A limpet gently grazes on encrusted algae growth"}]},
{title:"Why organisms settle on exhibit ledges",text:"The update proposed two possibilities for strong settlement on ledges: settlement cues and food. Coralline algae can act as a settlement cue for some local echinoderm larvae, helping indicate shallow coastal habitat. Ledges also provide food for grazers, omnivores and scavengers, while carnivores can find prey such as hydroids and small anemones. Skeleton shrimp can reproduce on exhibit because their larval stages remain in the mother’s brood pouch and emerge as miniature adults rather than requiring a pelagic stage.",photos:[{src:"IMG_2874.jpeg",caption:"An opalescent nudibranch cruising among small white anemones"},{src:"IMG_2876.jpeg",caption:"A small spotted triopha nudibranch crawling up the Monterey Bay Habitats exhibit window"}]},
{title:"Ledges as accessible observation stations",text:"Not every animal on a ledge is a volunteer organism. Monitoring ledges can reveal cryptic or temporary exhibit animals in places where guests can see them more easily. The update emphasized ledges as accessible observation sites for children and guests who may have difficulty standing or searching deeper into an exhibit.",photos:[{src:"IMG_2877.jpeg",caption:"A California sea hare on the edge of the ledge"},{src:"IMG_2878.jpeg",caption:"A painted greenling hiding behind kelp fronds on the second story window ledge of the Kelp Forest exhibit"},{src:"IMG_2882.jpeg",caption:"A California moray eel resting on the ledge with a rockfish pillow"},{src:"IMG_2883.jpeg",caption:"An older California sheephead female in the process of changing into a male"}]},
{title:"Camouflage, feeding and interpretation on the ledge",text:"The ledges offer opportunities to interpret adaptations. Limpets graze algae; falsejingles, chitons and some snails can become algae-encrusted and better camouflaged; aeolid nudibranchs such as opalescent nudibranchs resemble parts of their prey and can retain consumed stinging cells for defense. Fish using swim bladders must actively move down the water column to rest near a ledge. Close animals can also become live models for discussing fish morphology and habitat adaptations.",photos:[{src:"IMG_2884.jpeg",caption:"A false jingle with an algae encrusted hat"},{src:"IMG_2885.jpeg",caption:"A small flatfish on the ledge of the Kelp Forest exhibit"},{src:"IMG_2886.jpeg",caption:"A bat star on the ledge of the Kelp Forest exhibit"}]},
{title:"Why the open-sea system matters",text:"The update closed by emphasizing that these small ledge ecosystems are made possible by the aquarium’s open-sea system. Organisms arriving from Monterey Bay can create rich, changing communities that reflect the bay’s diversity and seasonal changes, rewarding careful observation by guides and guests."}
],highlight:null,qa:[],bay:["Humpback whales","Blue whale","Minke whales","Orcas (CA113s, CA20 aka Glacier, CA177, & currently unidentified pod seen 8/12)","Risso’s dolphins","Long-beaked common dolphins","Southern sea otters","California sea lions","Harbor seals","Black-footed albatrosses","Sooty shearwaters","Pink-footed shearwaters","Buller’s shearwaters","Manx shearwater (rare vagrant!)","Northern fulmars","California brown pelicans","Common murres","Pigeon guillemots","Rhinoceros auklets","Brandt's cormorants","Pelagic cormorants","Western gulls","Heermann's gulls","California gulls","Elegant terns","Parasitic jaeger","Unidentified booby (either Nazca or masked)","Red-necked phalaropes","Northern anchovies (huge piles of bait visible near the surface)","Flying fish","Ocean sunfish"],resources:["Current Signup Genius","Upcoming Signup Genius","Shift Snack Signup","Naturalist Library","How to Log Into the Naturalist Library","Tidepool","How to Log Into Tidepool","MBARI Hydrophone"]},{id:"2026-08-13-19",date:"Aug 13–19, 2026",title:"Rare short-beaked common dolphins, humpback fingerprints & green falsejingles",summary:["Rare short-beaked common dolphins were seen close to the Monterey Bay coast","Humpback identification focused on fluke shape, markings, scars and notches","Regular humpbacks included Angelwing, Little Dimple and Checkmarks; Snowy Owl, Croc and Bread Knife were highlighted for distinctive flukes","Species Highlight: green falsejingle (Pododesmus macrochisma)"],observations:[{title:"Shift coverage and substitute reminder",text:"The Upcoming Signup Genius was posted. Guides were reminded to seek a substitute when they cannot make a shift, consider reciprocity when requesting coverage, and use the substitute list and guides from compatible shifts."},{title:"Rare short-beaked common dolphins",text:"Short-beaked common dolphins were reported close to the coast, an unusual overlap with the more frequently encountered long-beaked common dolphins. The source update explains the current taxonomy as Delphinus delphis ssp. delphis for the animals commonly called short-beaked common dolphins and Delphinus delphis ssp. bairdii for the animals commonly called long-beaked common dolphins."},{title:"Reading humpback whale tail flukes",text:"Humpbacks are identified primarily from the underside of their tail flukes. Size, shape, black-and-white pattern, scars, notches and the trailing edge combine like a fingerprint. The update highlighted Snowy Owl (CRC-15242), named for an owl-like face in the white patch of the left fluke; Croc (CRC-10725), named for a crocodile-mouth shape on the right fluke; and Bread Knife (CRC-16963), named for the distinctive trailing edge. Regular whales Angelwing, Little Dimple and Checkmarks were also present.",photos:[{src:"IMG_2773.jpeg",caption:"How to read a humpback whale’s tailfin — image from Oceanic Society"},{src:"IMG_2774.jpeg",caption:"Snowy Owl, CRC-15242 — owl-like face in the white patch of the left fluke"},{src:"IMG_2775.jpeg",caption:"Croc, CRC-10725 — crocodile-mouth shape on the right fluke"},{src:"IMG_2776.jpeg",caption:"Bread Knife, CRC-16963 — distinctive trailing edge"}]}],highlight:{name:"Green falsejingle",scientific:"Pododesmus macrochisma",url:"../green-falsejingle/index.html",intro:"A slender cementing bivalve also called a rock oyster or mermaid’s toenail. The permanent biology belongs in the dedicated Green Falsejingle Species Guide; this Field Note preserves when and why it was highlighted.",facts:["Length: usually 2–4 in; up to about 5 in","Diet: plankton","Predators include sunflower, ochre, mottled and rainbow stars, giant Pacific octopus and black oystercatcher","Habitat: shallow rocky and shale reefs and artificial structures such as docks","Depth: commonly 0–100 ft; reported to about 300 ft","Range: Southern California (arguably Baja California) to Alaska and Alaska to Japan","Estimated lifespan: 3–10 years","Conservation status: Not Evaluated"],topics:["Bivalve larval life cycle","Pediveliger settlement","Cementing with a byssus plug","Filter feeding and water clarity","Nutrient and heavy-metal removal","Habitat for encrusting organisms","Predation by giant Pacific octopus","Biofouling communities"]},qa:[],bay:["Humpback whales","Blue whales","Orca (CA50B / Jimmy)","Risso’s dolphins","Long-beaked common dolphins","Short-beaked common dolphins (rare sighting)","Southern sea otters","California sea lions","Harbor seals","Black-footed albatrosses","Sooty shearwaters","Pink-footed shearwaters","Buller’s shearwaters","Northern fulmars","California brown pelicans","Common murres","Pigeon guillemots","Rhinoceros auklets","Brandt's cormorants","Pelagic cormorants","Western gulls","Heermann's gulls","California gulls","Bonaparte's gulls","Elegant terns","Northern anchovies","Flying fish","Ocean sunfish","Moon jellies","Great white shark"]},{id:"2026-08-20-26",date:"Aug 20–26, 2026",title:"Timber Fire, marine impacts & scarred short-beaked common dolphins",summary:["Short-beaked common dolphins increasingly seen around Monterey Bay","Tooth-rake, remora and probable Pacific lamprey scarring observed","Topic Highlight: how wildfire smoke, ash, runoff and fire suppressants can affect the ocean","Bay Watch included humpback and blue whales plus both common-dolphin species"],observations:[{title:"Rotation and hard-station reminder",text:"The update reminded volunteers to keep all hard stations staffed through every block, alternate hard and soft assignments when possible, arrive 3–5 minutes early when moving from a soft station to a hard station, and call Center for an IP specialist if relief is five minutes late or more."},{title:"Short-beaked common dolphin scarring",text:"More short-beaked common dolphins were reported around the bay. They showed extensive tooth-rake marks as well as remora scarring and small circular scars interpreted in the source update as most likely from Pacific lamprey; the scars were considered too small for cookiecutter shark bites."},{title:"Topic Highlight — Wildfires and ocean health",text:"Prompted by the visible Timber Fire in Big Sur, the update explored how wildfire smoke, ash, post-fire runoff and fire-suppression chemicals can affect coastal ecosystems. It discussed particulate matter, reduced sunlight, pH changes, nutrients, heavy metals, PFAS, dissolved organic matter, plankton responses, harmful algal blooms, food-web effects and potential impacts on marine mammals. The update also described KelpFire, a research effort studying wildfire effects on California kelp ecosystems."},{title:"Fire suppressants and runoff",text:"The update described common fire-retardant ingredients and concerns about concentrated nutrients, ammonia, heavy metals and runoff. It emphasized a second pulse of material after winter rains wash ash-enriched soil into streams and the ocean, potentially encouraging plankton blooms or harmful algal blooms."},{title:"Marine mammals and cascading effects",text:"The source connected wildfire-driven changes with possible bioaccumulation, reduced immune response, habitat degradation, altered prey, stress and reproductive effects. It cited observations and research involving sea otters, sea lions, dolphins and whales while noting that ocean effects can be difficult to attribute to a single cause."}],highlight:{name:"Wildfires & Ocean Health",scientific:"Topic Highlight",url:"../wildfires-ocean-health/index.html",intro:"A dedicated concept guide preserves the reusable science from this week's Timber Fire topic while this Field Note preserves the original date and context.",facts:["Smoke and ash can carry organic and inorganic particulates into coastal waters","Haze and ash can reduce sunlight available to phytoplankton","Ash and runoff can add nutrients, heavy metals and other contaminants","Large nutrient pulses can alter plankton communities and may contribute to harmful algal blooms","Fire suppressants can create downstream ecological effects when concentrated or washed into water"],topics:["Smoke ecology","Ash deposition","Dissolved organic matter","Phytoplankton and zooplankton","PFAS and heavy metals","Fire retardants","Post-fire runoff","Harmful algal blooms","Kelp forests","Marine mammal health"]},qa:[],bay:["Humpback whales","Blue whales","Risso’s dolphins","Long-beaked & short-beaked common dolphins","Harbor porpoises","Southern sea otters","California sea lions","Harbor seals","Black-footed albatrosses","Sooty shearwaters","Pink-footed shearwaters","Buller’s shearwaters","Northern fulmars","California brown pelicans","Common murres","Pigeon guillemots","Rhinoceros auklets","Brandt's cormorants","Pelagic cormorants","Western gulls","Heermann's gulls","California gulls","Elegant terns","Caspian terns","Pomarine jaeger","Parasitic jaeger","Brown booby","Northern anchovies"]},{id:"2026-08-27-09-02",date:"Aug 27–Sept 2, 2026",title:"Nazca booby, anchovy silver flashes & gooseneck barnacles",summary:["About 20+ humpback whales foraging around Monterey Canyon","Rare Nazca booby circled the boat at very close range","Anchovies produced dramatic silver flashes while escaping feeding whales","Species Highlight: gooseneck barnacle (Pollicipes polymerus)"],observations:[{title:"Humpbacks building toward peak season",text:"With calm, warm early-fall weather on the water, humpback activity appeared to be increasing. About 20 or more whales were reported around the canyon. Individuals named in the source update included Checkmarks, Chex Mix, Angel Wing, Diablito, Freckles, Danger Zone, Pacman, Frosty, Requiem, Tidal, Soqeul, Nerd Girl, Stego, Akiko and Caraxes."},{title:"Rare Nazca booby encounter",text:"A rare neotropical Nazca booby circled the boat for more than five minutes and repeatedly approached at very close range, at times coming too close to fit in the camera frame. The encounter was described as an unusually friendly vagrant."},{title:"Anchovy silver flashes",text:"Large schools of anchovies were seen leaping from the water en masse as humpbacks fed beneath them. The sudden surface disturbance sounded like rain and lasted only a moment before a whale often surfaced with its mouth open. Anchovies that escaped the whales were then available to other predators and scavengers."},{title:"Western gull feeding",text:"Western gulls were observed taking advantage of the anchovy activity, including a gull flying away with an anchovy it had caught."}],highlight:{name:"Gooseneck barnacle",scientific:"Pollicipes polymerus",url:"../gooseneck-barnacle/index.html",intro:"Species Highlight from the Aug 27–Sept 2 Naturalist update. The permanent biology belongs in the dedicated Gooseneck Barnacle Species Guide; this Field Note preserves when and why the animal was highlighted.",facts:["Length: up to 8 in","Diet: plankton","Predators: glaucous-winged gull, ochre star, six-rayed stars (Leptasterias sp.), black oystercatchers and humans","Habitat: intertidal rocky shore","Depth: 0–100 ft","Range: Alaska to Baja California, Mexico","Lifespan: up to 20 years","Conservation status: Not Evaluated"],topics:["Nauplius and cyprid larval stages","Settlement using cement glands and a cement duct","Dense rosettes and cross-fertilization","Cirri and filter feeding","Flexible stalk adaptation to heavy surf","Reproduction and broods","Human harvest and culinary use"]},qa:[],bay:["Humpback whales","Risso’s dolphins","Long-beaked common dolphins","Harbor porpoises","Southern sea otters","California sea lions","Harbor seals","Elephant seal (juvenile on San Carlos)","Black-footed albatrosses","Sooty shearwaters","Pink-footed shearwaters","Buller’s shearwaters","Northern fulmars","Black storm-petrel","California brown pelicans","Common murres","Pigeon guillemots","Rhinoceros auklets","Brandt's cormorants","Pelagic cormorants","Western gulls","Heermann's gulls","California gulls","Sabine's gulls","Elegant terns","Caspian terns","Pomarine jaeger","Parasitic jaeger","Nazca booby","Northern anchovies"]},{id:"2026-09-03-09",date:"Sept 3–9, 2026",title:"Olive ridley visitor, shark parthenogenesis & returning Risso’s dolphins",summary:["Rare olive ridley sea turtle near Moss Landing","20–40+ humpbacks; groups up to roughly 13–15","Risso’s dolphins returning as common-dolphin activity eased","Large numbers of red-necked phalaropes and common murre dad–chick pairs"],observations:[{title:"Olive ridley sea turtle near Moss Landing",text:"Katie spotted an olive ridley sea turtle near Moss Landing. The note explains that olive ridleys are warm-water turtles and that previous northern visitors have sometimes suffered cold stun. During El Niño conditions they can range unusually far north; warming water after the upwelling season may make Monterey Bay temporarily more hospitable.",photos:[{src:"IMG_3321.jpeg",caption:"Katie’s picture of the olive ridley"}]},{title:"Parthenogenesis in sharks and rays",text:"The Tennessee Aquarium reported an epaulette shark that had lived without a male for years producing a fertile egg case, similar to the Monterey Bay Aquarium swell shark case. The note discusses parthenogenesis across sharks and other elasmobranchs and lists blacktip, bonnethead, common smooth-hound, dusky smooth-hound, epaulette, swell, whitespotted bamboo, whitetip reef and zebra sharks as species with recorded cases under human care."},{title:"Humpback whale activity and known individuals",text:"Trips were seeing roughly 20 to more than 40 humpbacks, sometimes concentrated within about a quarter mile; the largest fluid group reported this week was roughly 13–15 whales. Digital whale cards created using Happywhale identification helped identify individuals seen on recent trips.",photos:[{src:"IMG_3323.jpeg",caption:"Sarafinny — humpback identification card."},{src:"IMG_3325.jpeg",caption:"Snowy Owl aka Trick-or-Treat — humpback identification card."},{src:"IMG_3326.jpeg",caption:"Stitches — humpback identification card."},{src:"IMG_3327.jpeg",caption:"Chex Mix — humpback identification card."},{src:"IMG_3328.jpeg",caption:"Mitten — humpback identification card."},{src:"IMG_3329.jpeg",caption:"Requiem — humpback identification card."}]},{title:"Risso’s dolphins return",text:"Risso’s dolphins were being seen again as the large summer groups of common dolphins became less dominant. The note also mentions reports of more market squid.",photos:[{src:"IMG_3330.jpeg",caption:"Risso’s dolphin in Monterey Bay"}]},{title:"Red-necked phalaropes",text:"Unusually large flocks of red-necked phalaropes—sometimes 12–15 birds—were observed. Several birds were seen spinning on the water at once, a feeding behavior that helps bring prey toward the surface.",photos:[{src:"IMG_3331.jpeg",caption:"Red-necked phalaropes on Monterey Bay"}]},{title:"Common murre dads and chicks",text:"Hundreds of common murre father–chick pairs were scattered across the bay. Adult males were actively feeding chicks and teaching them to dive and forage; adults were observed surfacing with anchovies and calling for their chicks.",photos:[{src:"IMG_3332.jpeg",caption:"Common murre dad and chick pairs on Monterey Bay"}]},{title:"California sea lion diet",text:"A California sea lion was observed swallowing an unidentified skate, possibly a longnose skate based on pattern and location. The note emphasizes the sea lion’s broad diet, including schooling fish, rockfish, salmon, sharks, rays, skates, octopuses, market squid and crabs.",photos:[{src:"IMG_3333.jpeg",caption:"California sea lion swallowing an unidentified skate, possibly a longnose skate"}]},{title:"R/V David Packard",text:"Kailey photographed the R/V David Packard near the mouth of Monterey Submarine Canyon with the Moss Landing smokestacks behind the ship.",photos:[{src:"IMG_3334.jpeg",caption:"Kailey’s picture of the R/V David Packard"}]},{title:"No Bones Bioblitz — iNat Together",text:"An iNaturalist No Bones Bioblitz was announced for Sept 18–20 around Monterey Bay, inviting observations of accessible marine invertebrates from Santa Cruz through the Monterey Peninsula. The note frames coastal invertebrates as sensitive indicators of warming conditions and explains the value of observations during a strong El Niño for comparing short-term marine heatwaves with longer-term warming.",photos:[{src:"IMG_3335.jpeg",caption:"QR code linking to the No Bones Bioblitz iNaturalist project"}]},{title:"Ghost redwoods at Henry Cowell",text:"Albino or ghost redwoods cannot produce chlorophyll and survive by root-grafting with nearby redwoods. The note discusses research suggesting their needles can accumulate substantially more heavy metals such as cadmium, copper and nickel, potentially reducing exposure for surrounding chlorophyll-producing trees.",photos:[{src:"IMG_3336.jpeg",caption:"Ghost redwood at Henry Cowell"}]}],highlight:null,qa:[],resources:["Current Signup Genius","Substitute Guide List (On Naturalist Library)","Shift Snack Signup","Naturalist Library","How to Log Into the Naturalist Library","Tidepool","How to Log Into Tidepool","MBARI Hydrophone"],bay:["Humpback whales","Orcas (CA51A & CA27B pods)","Risso’s dolphins","Long-beaked common dolphins","Harbor porpoises","Southern sea otters","California sea lions","Harbor seals","Elephant seal (San Carlos Beach)","Black-footed albatrosses","Sooty shearwaters","Pink-footed shearwaters","Black-vented shearwaters","Northern fulmars","California brown pelicans","Common murres","Pigeon guillemots","Rhinoceros auklets","Tufted puffin (rare sighting)","Brandt's cormorants","Pelagic cormorants","Western gulls","Heermann's gulls","California gulls","Sabine’s gulls","Common terns","Caspian terns","Parasitic jaegers","Pomarine jaegers","Red-necked phalaropes","Red phalarope","Northern anchovies","Moon jellies","Olive ridley sea turtle (rare sighting)","Great white shark"]},{id:"2026-09-24-30",date:"Sept 24–30, 2026",title:"Peak humpbacks, Ventana returns & flamboyant cuttlefish",summary:["50–60 humpbacks around Moss Landing","100–1,000+ common dolphins","Ventana returned with a healed back","Common murre dad–chick pairs dense on the water"],observations:[{title:"Into the Deep station note",text:"The warming rest at the Isopod station was reported not working. Staff were aware and a repair request had been submitted; larger Into the Deep projects might take precedence."},{title:"Peak whale season",text:"An average of 50–60 humpbacks were reported around Moss Landing, with large groups of common dolphins—both long-beaked and short-beaked—moving through the bay."},{title:"Pelagic birds",text:"Sooty, pink-footed and black-vented shearwaters were reported, along with stray black storm-petrels. Hundreds of common murre dad–chick pairs were noted."},{title:"Ventana",text:"Ventana, a humpback first noted in Monterey Bay in October 2024, returned after being seen the prior October with deep wounds attributed in the field note to a recent vessel strike. The new observation described the back as appearing recovered."}],highlight:{name:"Flamboyant cuttlefish",scientific:"Ascarosepion pfefferi",url:"../flamboyant-cuttlefish/index.html",intro:"Species Highlight for the upcoming Ocean in Motion reopening. The durable species material belongs in the dedicated species guide; this Field Note preserves when and why it was highlighted.",facts:["Length: 2.5–3 in","Diet: crustaceans & small bony fish","Habitat: coral reefs; sand or mud flats","Depth: 10–280 ft; commonly 10–30 ft","Range: Indonesia, Malaysia, Papua New Guinea & northern Australia","Lifespan: commonly 18–24 months; semelparous","IUCN: Data Deficient"],topics:["Life cycle & reproduction","Feeding tentacles","Chromatophores, iridophores & leucophores","Walking behavior & cuttlebone","Cephalopod anatomy & cognition","Tetrodotoxin / aposematism","Biomimicry"]},qa:[],bay:["Humpback whales","Orcas (CA51As with CA50B; CA27 + CA27E pods)","Risso’s dolphins","Common dolphins (long-beaked & short-beaked)","Bottlenose dolphins","Harbor porpoises","Southern sea otters","California sea lions","Harbor seals","Elephant seal pups","Sooty shearwaters","Pink-footed shearwaters","Black-vented shearwaters","Black storm-petrel","California brown pelicans","Common murres","Pigeon guillemots","Rhinoceros auklets","Brandt's cormorants","Pelagic cormorants","Western gulls","Heermann's gulls","California gulls","Sabine’s gulls","Common terns","Caspian terns","Parasitic jaegers","Ocean sunfish"]},{id:"2026-09-10-16",date:"Sept 10–16, 2026",title:"Whale skin, echolocation & a busy Monterey Bay",summary:["30–50 humpbacks on the water","200+ long-beaked common dolphins near Del Monte Beach","CA51As around Moss Landing","Large humpback feeding groups of 6–12"],observations:[{title:"Seaside sea otters",text:"A pair of Southern sea otters were observed drifting together about half a mile off Seaside.",photos:[{src:"IMG_2345.jpeg",caption:"A pair of Southern sea otters drifting together about ½ mile off Seaside."}]},{title:"Friendly humpback encounter",text:"One of several close humpback encounters reported during the week.",photos:[{src:"IMG_2346.jpeg",caption:"One of several friendly humpback whale encounters reported recently."}]},{title:"Humpback feeding",text:"Groups of 6–12 humpbacks were reported feeding together, with occasional vertical lunge-feeding when anchovies were shallow. As surface water warms, anchovies may seek deeper, cooler water.",photos:[{src:"IMG_2347.jpeg",caption:"Large humpback feeding group; shallow anchovies can encourage vertical lunge-feeding."}]},{title:"Known individuals — Jimmy",text:"Jimmy (CA50B) closely associates with the CA51As and was described in the field note as the uncle of matriarch Aurora (CA51A).",photos:[{src:"IMG_2348.jpeg",caption:"Jimmy, CA50B."}]},{title:"Known individuals — Aurora family",text:"Aurora (CA51A), Dipper (CA51A3), Cassiopeia (CA51A4), and Dipper’s first calf Nova (CA51A3A).",photos:[{src:"IMG_2349.jpeg",caption:"Aurora with Dipper, Cassiopeia and Nova."}]},{title:"Known individuals — Andi & Astrid",text:"Andi (CA51A2), Aurora’s second living daughter, with her first calf Astrid (CA51A2A).",photos:[{src:"IMG_2352.jpeg",caption:"Andi (CA51A2) and Astrid (CA51A2A)."}]},{title:"Long-beaked common dolphins",text:"Large groups of long-beaked common dolphins were observed in calm, clear Monterey Bay water.",photos:[{src:"IMG_2353.jpeg",caption:"Long-beaked common dolphins in calm, clear water."}]}],highlight:null,qa:[{q:"Why can’t whales molt well in cold water?",short:"In cold water, whales can reduce blood flow near the skin to conserve heat. Reduced skin perfusion and turnover can slow epidermal shedding. Warm-water movements can increase skin metabolism and facilitate molt; researchers have proposed epidermal maintenance as one possible driver of some whale movements.",photos:[{src:"IMG_2354.jpeg",caption:"Humpback whale tail with gray patches of dead skin."},{src:"IMG_2355.jpeg",caption:"Antarctic killer-whale diatom and skin-shedding graphic by Emma Luck."}],tags:["whales","molting","migration","physiology"]},{q:"Why don’t whales get prune-y?",short:"Human water-induced wrinkling is an active autonomic vasoconstriction response, especially in the thick skin of fingers and toes. Cetaceans do not use that same specialized response, and their skin is adapted for continuous aquatic life.",tags:["whales","skin","physiology"]},{q:"Did baleen whales lose echolocation?",short:"Current evidence supports sophisticated biosonar evolving in the toothed-whale lineage after toothed and baleen whales diverged. Baleen whales specialize more strongly in lower-frequency acoustic communication and hearing than odontocetes.",photos:[{src:"IMG_2359.jpeg",caption:"Odontocete echolocation anatomy: sound production through the melon and sound reception through the lower jaw."}],tags:["whales","echolocation","evolution","sound"]}],deepDive:{title:"Cetacean backbones: speed vs. maneuverability",text:"The update highlighted research connecting cetacean vertebral number and regionalization with locomotor demands. Offshore forms can show spinal patterns associated with high-speed swimming, while riverine/coastal forms can emphasize maneuverability.",photos:[{src:"IMG_2360.jpeg",caption:"Cetacean vertebral regionalization from shallow-water to open-ocean forms. Image by Amandine Gillet."}]},bay:["Humpback whales","Orcas (CA51A & CA140B pods)","Risso’s dolphins","Long-beaked common dolphins","Harbor porpoises","Southern sea otters","California sea lions","Harbor seals","Sooty shearwaters","Pink-footed shearwaters","Black-vented shearwaters","Northern fulmars","California brown pelicans","Common murres","Pigeon guillemots","Rhinoceros auklets","Brandt's cormorants","Pelagic cormorants","Western gulls","Heermann's gulls","California gulls","Sabine’s gulls","Common terns","Caspian terns","Parasitic jaegers","Pomarine jaegers","Red-necked phalaropes","Northern anchovies","Moon jellies","Great white shark (unconfirmed report noted in source email)"]}];const RESOURCES=["Current Signup Genius","Substitute Guide List (Naturalist Library)","Shift Snack Signup","Naturalist Library","How to Log Into the Naturalist Library","Tidepool","How to Log Into Tidepool","MBARI Hydrophone"];